## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cross-account IAM, KMS usage, and end-to-end data flow in a real-world streaming pipeline, plus a concrete test plan.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account trust with IAM roles and principals
- Kinesis PutRecords permissions and TLS enforcement
- SSE-KMS with a CMK in the data-owner account
- Lambda assuming a restricted role using session tags for fine-grained access
- Test strategy: canaries, revocation timing, and anomaly detection

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/ProducerRole"},
      "Action": "kinesis:PutRecords",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:REGION:ACCOUNT_B:stream/STREAM_NAME",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/partner": "Acme"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt", "kms:Decrypt", "kms:GenerateDataKey*"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:REGION:ACCOUNT_B:key/CMK_ID",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"kms:ViaService": "kinesis.REGION.amazonaws.com"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate the CMK with minimal disruption?
- How would you monitor PutRecords failures and automatically alert on unauthorized access attempts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement real-time, policy-driven containment in a multi-tenant data lake, focusing on automated incident response, policy-as-code, and auditability.

## Key Concepts

- Lake Formation permissions and ABAC via IAM tags
- CloudTrail + EventBridge for event-driven enforcement
- Data quarantine patterns and catalog synchronization
- GitOps workflows for policy deployment and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-Lambda: react to leakage event
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  // parse tenant and offending principal
  const tenant = event.detail.tenant;
  const principal = event.detail.principal;
  // revoke Lake Formation permissions for offending principal on tenant data
  await revokeLFPermissions(tenant, principal);
  // quarantine data and update catalog
  await quarantineTenantData(tenant);
  // push policy changes to GitOps repo and notify
  await pushPolicyGitOps(tenant);
  return { status: 'ok' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test latency and ensure no data leakage during rollback?
- How would you handle false positives and maintain tenant trust while preserving audit trails?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bucket policies, per-prefix isolation, TLS enforcement, and practical test plans.

## Key Concepts
- S3 bucket policies, IAM roles, TLS enforcement, per-prefix access control
- Canary tests to verify allowed and denied actions

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::acct:role/TeamARole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/teamA/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::acct:role/TeamBRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/teamB/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs","arn:aws:s3:::customer-logs/*"],
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "false"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a new team without duplicating policy blocks?
- How would you monitor and rotate the IAM roles used here?

