## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, compliant multi-tenant real-time pipeline with data residency requirements, cross-account access, and disaster recovery.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant data locality
- Exactly-once delivery semantics across streaming and storage
- Cross-account IAM with STS and least privilege
- DR planning across regions, cost-aware choices
- Observability with CloudWatch, X-Ray

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: idempotent event handler skeleton
async function handleEvent(evt){
  const key = evt.tenant_id + ':' + evt.event_id;
  const exists = await db.get(key);
  if(exists) return; // idempotent
  await db.put(key, true);
  // process
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate RPO/RTO and perform canary DR tests?
- What are trade-offs between KDS and MSK in cost, latency, and reliability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end AWS streaming design, data locality, replay semantics, and observability across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality, replay, idempotency, cross-region replication, shard throttling, and cost trade-offs.
- Durability vs latency in KDS/Kafka vs Firehose patterns.
- Observability via CloudWatch, X-Ray, and structured metrics.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent sink to DynamoDB
async function sinkEvent(event){
  const key = event.tenantId + '#' + event.id;
  await dynamo.put({
    TableName: 'TenantEvents',
    Item: { PK: key, ...event },
    ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(PK)'
  }).catch(err => {
    if (err.code === 'ConditionalCheckFailedException') return; // idempotent retry
    throw err
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle shard throttling and bursty traffic?
- How would you onboard new tenants without impacting latency or isolation?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical federation, per-service IAM scoping, and operational controls for external partner access. It requires concrete policy boundaries, token lifetimes, and revocation/audit mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- OIDC federation with API Gateway
- STS: AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
- Per-service IAM roles and least privilege
- Trust policies and cross-account boundaries
- Token lifetimes and revocation workflows
- CloudTrail/audit trails and alerting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Trust policy for the role assumed via web identity
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Federated": "arn:aws:iam::000000000000:oidc-provider/partner-idp"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"token_use": "id"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Per-service policy snippet (example for S3 access)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::tenant-<tenant_id>-data/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Rollout Plan

- Integrate partner IdP with AWS IAM OIDC provider
- Create per-service roles and API Gateway authorizers
- Set TTLs to 3600s and enable automatic revocation
- Enable CloudTrail data events and alerting
- Onboard/offboard workflow and monitoring
